1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,862
A camera can record an incident without getting help to the right person.

2
00:00:04,862 --> 00:00:11,602
EduSphere Safety connects school camera events to a staff response, with evidence and a clear record of who acted.

3
00:00:12,250 --> 00:00:16,313
The edge agent decodes video, tracks people, and applies scheduled rules.

4
00:00:16,313 --> 00:00:18,160
A cloud API stores incidents.

5
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:23,700
The staff console combines evidence review, local Meta Llama briefings, and a recorded response workflow.

6
00:00:24,250 --> 00:00:28,453
This is the running application with a real PostgreSQL database.

7
00:00:28,453 --> 00:00:31,816
We sign in as the school safety officer.

8
00:00:31,816 --> 00:00:37,700
The overview shows enrolled devices and open incidents, while diagnostics identifies camera coverage gaps.

9
00:00:38,250 --> 00:00:42,426
A school defines a restricted zone and an after-hours schedule.

10
00:00:42,426 --> 00:00:48,272
This editor stores the polygon, confidence threshold, persistence, and cooldown in a versioned bundle.

11
00:00:48,272 --> 00:00:53,700
Persistence requires multiple frames, so one isolated detection does not trigger this rule.

12
00:00:54,250 --> 00:00:58,486
This reproducible demo uses synthetic footage and replayed detections.

13
00:00:58,486 --> 00:01:07,899
The real FFmpeg decoder, tracker, and rule engine process sixty frames and write one zone event to the persistent outbox.

14
00:01:07,899 --> 00:01:11,194
This verifies pipeline behavior, not detector accuracy.

15
00:01:12,250 --> 00:01:17,166
A demo handoff sends the edge outbox event to the real API.

16
00:01:17,166 --> 00:01:20,853
Retrying the same event returns the same incident identifier.

17
00:01:20,853 --> 00:01:24,130
Staff open that single incident from the inbox.

18
00:01:26,250 --> 00:01:32,325
We upload the synthetic clip through the evidence API and open it from the incident.

19
00:01:32,325 --> 00:01:36,780
Playback is authorized through a short-lived link, and access is logged.

20
00:01:36,780 --> 00:01:42,450
This supplied test clip gives staff context; automatic live-camera clip extraction remains future work.

21
00:01:44,250 --> 00:01:48,506
Now we ask Meta Llama to draft an incident briefing.

22
00:01:48,506 --> 00:01:53,614
It runs locally and receives only authorized event metadata, never camera footage.

23
00:01:53,614 --> 00:01:58,722
The structured result gives staff a summary, review steps, and explicit unknowns.

24
00:01:58,722 --> 00:02:02,127
It cannot change the incident or take action.

25
00:02:02,127 --> 00:02:05,106
Staff verify the draft against the evidence.

26
00:02:06,250 --> 00:02:11,236
The officer acknowledges the incident, adds an investigation note, and resolves it.

27
00:02:11,236 --> 00:02:15,390
Each transition checks the current version to prevent conflicting updates.

28
00:02:15,390 --> 00:02:18,299
The database records the actor and activity.

29
00:02:18,299 --> 00:02:23,700
Acknowledgement also cancels pending escalation, connecting the alert to an accountable staff response.

30
00:02:24,250 --> 00:02:27,388
Notification policy sets escalation recipients and delays.

31
00:02:27,388 --> 00:02:32,768
The worker writes email to a local pickup directory in this demo.

32
00:02:32,768 --> 00:02:37,700
Diagnostics shows evidence access and untested cameras, making monitoring gaps visible.

33
00:02:38,250 --> 00:02:44,458
All five hundred and nine existing tests pass, including PostgreSQL integration tests.

34
00:02:44,458 --> 00:02:51,700
Next come real-camera validation, measured detector benchmarks, automatic edge delivery, and production sign-in integration.

35
00:02:52,250 --> 00:02:56,194
EduSphere Safety makes the response part of the record.

36
00:02:56,194 --> 00:02:59,700
It assists school staff; it never replaces supervision.
